---
// Section 04 · The system. One machine in three named parts.
//
// This section deliberately breaks the page's section-opening grammar. Every
// other section opened eyebrow → full-width hairline → heading at columns 1-8
// → body at 1-8, five in a row, which left every section's content in the left
// half of the grid and the right half empty top to bottom. The direction
// offers "columns 1-7 or 4-12" and only the first was ever in use, so
// "asymmetric by default" had become "left-aligned always" — symmetry of a
// different kind.
//
// Here the eyebrow, heading and intro sit in a rail at columns 1-7 and the
// three part rows run at 7-13, so the section fills the right half of the grid
// that the rest of the page never touches. Columns 1-7 is the placement the
// direction offers alongside 4-12; it was simply never used.
//
// The opening hairline is vertical rather than horizontal — it is the rail's
// right edge, and it runs the full height of the rows because the rail is a
// stretched grid item. That is the break. The rule still separates two zones
// the way a rule on a drawing does; it separates them across the page instead
// of down it, which is why the horizontal opening rule is gone rather than
// duplicated.
//
// This is one of exactly two sections that break the pattern — section 08 is
// the other. Sections 01, 02 and 07 keep the established opening, so the two
// breaks read as decisions rather than as inconsistency.
//
// The rows are bounded top and bottom: a hairline above each row, and a
// closing hairline under the last, carried by the container. The earlier
// version had no closing rule on the grounds that it would separate the
// section from its own padding. With the rail in place it separates something
// real — it closes the block the rail's vertical rule opens, and the three
// parts read as one bounded assembly rather than as three loose rows.
//
// That closing rule is also what makes this section distinguishable from 07
// without relying on gap size. Four structural differences now, not one:
// rail versus on-top heading, stacked versus three-column rows, bounded versus
// open-ended, --size-h2 part names versus --size-h3 step titles.
//
// Zero --signal-500 in this section. It is explanatory, not persuasive, and
// there is nothing here that is load-bearing enough to mark. It also buys
// headroom: section 02 spends its one accent at its closing block, directly
// above this section's opening.
import Eyebrow from './Eyebrow.astro';
import DisplayHeading from './DisplayHeading.astro';
import ServiceRow from './ServiceRow.astro';
---

<section id="the-system" class="the-system">
	<div class="content">
		<div class="system-rail">
			<Eyebrow label="fig. 04 / the system" />

			<DisplayHeading as="h2" class="system-heading">Three parts. One machine.</DisplayHeading>

			<p class="system-intro">
				Most shops sell you a website, or a CRM, or an answering service, and leave you
				to connect them. Nothing connects. The lead comes in one door and dies in
				another. We build the whole thing as one system, wired together, or we don't
				build it. We don't start by selling you a website. We start with the customers
				you already have.
			</p>
		</div>

		<div class="system-rows">
			<ServiceRow
				index="01"
				name="The front door"
				closing="Not a brochure. The thing that puts you in front of someone searching at 9pm."
			>
				A website built to be found. Real local SEO structure, service-area pages for
				every town you work in, fast enough to load on a job site with two bars.
			</ServiceRow>

			<ServiceRow
				index="02"
				name="The engine"
				closing="Built in GoHighLevel, configured around your jobs and your pricing. You own it."
			>
				Every lead lands in one place with the whole history attached — where they came
				from, what they asked for, what you quoted, what happened. Pipelines, automatic
				follow-up on quotes that went quiet, review requests that go out when the job is
				done instead of three weeks later.
			</ServiceRow>

			<ServiceRow
				index="03"
				name="The mouth"
				closing="Answers every call, including the one at 9pm on a Sunday."
			>
				An answering service takes a message. Ours asks what the job is, whether it's in
				your area, and when you can come look at it — then puts it on your calendar and
				drops the record in the pipeline.
			</ServiceRow>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	.the-system {
		padding-block: var(--space-3xl);
	}

	/* Stretched to the height of .system-rows by the grid's default
	   align-items, which is what makes the border read as a rail running the
	   length of the block rather than as an underline on the intro. */
	/* Six columns, not four or five. Measured at 1440: "One machine." sets 415px
	   wide at --size-display, and the headline has to break on its own sentence
	   or it reads as "Three / parts. One / machine." — a break mid-sentence,
	   which is the exact failure site-structure.md recorded when a left rail was
	   tried on section 07. Four columns gave 317px of text and five gave 410px,
	   both short. Six gives 504px, which clears 415 by enough that the break
	   survives a font fallback.

	   --size-display caps at 55.25px above a 1381px viewport and the content
	   column caps at 1200px, so this is the tightest the pairing ever gets;
	   below 1381px the type shrinks on 4vw while the rail stays fixed. */
	.system-rail {
		grid-column: 1 / 7;
		border-right: var(--rule-weight) solid var(--rule);
		padding-inline-end: var(--space-lg);
	}

	.the-system :global(.system-heading) {
		margin-block: var(--space-md) var(--space-md);
	}

	.system-intro {
		color: var(--text-body);
	}

	/* The closing hairline lives on the container, not on the last row — one
	   declaration instead of piercing ServiceRow's scoped styles. Rows are
	   contiguous; their own top borders do the separating. */
	.system-rows {
		grid-column: 7 / 13;
		border-bottom: var(--rule-weight) solid var(--rule);
	}

	@media (max-width: 700px) {
		/* The rail becomes a header again below 700px — there is no room for
		   two columns, and a vertical rule with nothing beside it separates
		   nothing. No horizontal rule replaces it: the first row's own
		   border-top already sits on that exact boundary, and stacking a rail
		   border on top of it draws the same separation twice. */
		.system-rail {
			grid-column: 1 / 13;
			border-right: none;
			padding-inline-end: 0;
			padding-block-end: var(--space-lg);
		}

		.system-rows {
			grid-column: 1 / 13;
		}
	}
</style>
